{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And You:</b> The reason is that <b>ואתה</b> is without change; therefore the word <b>תשב</b> — for every vowel change is a change. Likewise <b>כסאך</b>, which is in the heavens. And the meaning: perhaps You will establish in me a good spirit from You, and let them not soon come to an end."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 102, :notes {0 "The argument hinges on a vowel point in תשב; 'without change' likely refers to the form being pointed without vowel alteration expected under some other reading. The antecedent of 'them' in 'let them not come to an end' is ambiguous in the Hebrew and may refer to the psalmist's days or strength."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:37:50.212986528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}